<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><span class="status-pill completed">Completed</span><div class="eyebrow">Computer Vision · CNN · ResNet18 · Generalisation</div><h1>MonReader — Robust Page-Turn Classification</h1><p class="section-lead">A completed computer-vision study asking whether page-turn detection still works on later frames and previously unseen recordings—not only on a familiar dataset split.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="container content">
<section class="story-section"><h2>Project at a glance</h2><div class="story-grid"><div class="story-step"><strong>1 · Objective</strong><p>Assess whether page-turn detection remains reliable on later frames and previously unseen recordings rather than only on a familiar dataset split.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>2 · Data</strong><p>2,989 labelled flip/notflip frames from 117 reconstructed recordings, with source and temporal splits designed to test generalisation.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>3 · Method</strong><p>Compare tuned Scratch CNN models with ImageNet-pretrained ResNet18 across four historical evaluation regimes and a separate progressive labelled-history study.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>4 · Evaluation</strong><p>Use source-shift and temporal-shift tests, source-video bootstrap uncertainty, failure analysis and a fixed late-frame cohort for the progressive study.</p></div><div class="story-step story-impact"><strong>5 · Outcome</strong><p>ResNet18 was stronger across the historical regimes, but performance declined from 0.995 Macro F1 on the easiest regime to 0.877 under combined source and temporal shift, supporting controlled evaluation rather than unrestricted deployment.</p></div></div></section>
<h2>Context and objective</h2>
<p>The research question is whether a model can detect page flips reliably in later frames and previously unseen recordings—not only in the original dataset split. The practical concern is that a hands-free reading system can appear strong on familiar frames while still missing page turns under changed recording conditions.</p>

<h2>Data and constraints</h2>
<p>The dataset contains <strong>2,989 labelled flip/notflip frames from 117 reconstructed recordings</strong>. Frame chronology comes from filenames rather than verified capture timestamps. In the historical ST test, 24 entirely withheld recordings contained 610 frames; their earlier frames were context only and the final 121 frames formed the scored late-only test.</p>

<h2>Technical solution</h2>
<p>The Scratch CNN was tuned with four independent W&amp;B Bayesian sweeps, one per historical regime, with 30 qualifying trials per regime. A separate ImageNet-pretrained ResNet18 was trained for each regime.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>O</strong>: supplied source-joint split.</li>
<li><strong>S</strong>: unseen sources.</li>
<li><strong>T</strong>: later frames from familiar sources.</li>
<li><strong>ST</strong>: unseen sources combined with later-frame stress.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Evaluation and evidence</h2>
<p>Source-video bootstrap intervals use 5,000 resamples because nearby frames are correlated. Failure analysis tracks false negatives and false positives by frame, and Grad-CAM examples are used only as diagnostic visual evidence rather than causal proof. A separate completed eight-run ResNet18 study tests whether more observed chronological history improves performance on a fixed final 10% of frames.</p>

<h2>Key results</h2>
<ul>
<li>O Macro F1: Scratch CNN <strong>0.990</strong>; ResNet18 <strong>0.995</strong>.</li>
<li>S Macro F1: Scratch CNN <strong>0.440</strong>; ResNet18 <strong>0.958</strong>.</li>
<li>T Macro F1: Scratch CNN <strong>0.831</strong>; ResNet18 <strong>0.909</strong>.</li>
<li>ST Macro F1: Scratch CNN <strong>0.791</strong>; ResNet18 <strong>0.877</strong>.</li>
<li>The historical ST 95% source-video bootstrap interval was approximately <strong>0.747–0.972</strong>.</li>
<li>Historical ResNet18 T recorded <strong>27 missed flip frames and no false alarms</strong>; ST recorded <strong>14 missed flip frames and no false alarms</strong>.</li>
<li>In the progressive study, T Macro F1 increased from <strong>0.338 at 25%</strong> observed history to <strong>0.730 at 90%</strong>; ST moved from <strong>0.347</strong> to <strong>0.653</strong>, with a dip at 50%.</li>
<li>At 90% history, missed-flip rates were still <strong>47.1% for T</strong> and <strong>58.1% for ST</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Limitations and scope</h2>
<p>The study measures missed flip frames, not skipped-page events, customer outcomes or operating cost. Regimes use different test populations, so cross-regime score gaps are descriptive rather than controlled causal effects. The progressive study also does not establish that additional data caused the improvements or that online learning occurred.</p>

<h2>Practical relevance</h2>
<p>The recommendation is <strong>controlled evaluation, not unrestricted deployment</strong>. A deployment decision would require independent timestamped recordings with event-level page-turn labels, predefined tolerances for missed events and latency, and testing on untouched users, devices and recording conditions.</p>

<h2>What this case proves</h2>
<p>I can build and compare computer-vision models, design evaluation around realistic distribution shift, quantify uncertainty, investigate failure patterns and resist treating a near-perfect nominal score as sufficient evidence for release.</p>

